WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.570
Speaker 1: Let's go next to let's go to Travis
from Manila.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:05.990
Is this Manila in the Philippines?

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.520
Speaker 2: Yes, sir, can you hear me?

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.110
Speaker 1: Yes, I can.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.110
Who are you?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.110
You an expat?

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:16.090
Speaker 2: Yeah, I'm just here for work.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.480
OK.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.010
My question for you.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.160
And thank you for taking my call.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.350
Big fan pleasure considering and forgive me,
I don't have the pole in front of me, but

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:32.689
I just seen a poll that said something like
80 percent of Americans were just completely

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.620
done with any or all COVID restrictions.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.620
Yeah.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.510
Would it be wise politically for Democrats
to move in that direction?

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.520
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.630
And I think that basically they already are.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:52.180
I mean, I think the Biden White House hasn't
yet really put out anything specific about

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.950
like, we're going through the next phase here.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:00.079
But I think many states are thinking in that
direction, even like I heard from family in

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:05.830
Massachusetts that the school guidelines and
you know, it's a Republican governor in Massachusetts,

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.460
but it's a it's, you know, liberal legislature.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.620
They're going in the direction of normalizing
a lot of school staff.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.270
I think I don't think the risk is over to
any degree, but I do think

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.870
Speaker 2: that so either, you know, but but
to be clear, politically makes sense.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:20.870
Yeah.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.830
Speaker 1: You don't think it makes sense
to get rid of guidelines or you don't think

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.090
it makes sense to keep them.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.880
Speaker 2: I don't I don't think it makes
sense to keep them

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:29.880
Speaker 1: right, yeah.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:36.180
No, I'm increasingly of that mindset and part
of it is with cases going down so rapidly

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.240
and the belief that we're moving into the
endemic phase endemic doesn't mean mild necessary,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.240
necessarily.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.280
You can have something that's endemic and
serious.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.200
It's just a question of like we were talking
about with Vincent Racaniello two weeks ago.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:56.950
What milestone are we waiting for in terms
of Omicron is we're well beyond the peak and

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:01.260
masking and vaccines are widely available.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.310
Everybody sort of knows at this point the
level of risk that you have.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.509
It's a question of like, what's the next milestone
we're waiting for if we don't start easing

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.650
off of stuff now and making things more kind
of personal decisions?

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.450
Speaker 2: What do you think the answer to
that question is?

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:23.750
Speaker 1: I mean, I think it's if there is
another variant that makes cases spike and

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:29.291
it's not significantly milder, like if the
next variant truly is just a cold, then I

00:02:29.291 --> 00:02:31.040
think we don't do anything.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.611
But if there's a new variant that is just
as dangerous as the ones that we've had, you

00:02:36.611 --> 00:02:39.640
probably put some guidelines back on is my
sense.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.810
Speaker 2: OK, I appreciate the time, David,
thank you very much.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:03:28.810
Speaker 1: My pleasure, thank you so much

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:53.590
for the call.

